# Nonzero primes of a Weierstrass coordinate ring are points

Theorem statement: `P2M.Dup.WeierstrassCurve.Affine.CoordinateRing.exists_eq_XYIdeal`
(`73374138-0ba8-52af-862c-d0e1b7361336`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `3369a3dc-414c-5f31-8f50-f5222dd637e6`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $K$ in affine form,
with coordinate ring `W.CoordinateRing`, i.e. $K[X][Y]$ modulo the Weierstrass polynomial $W(X,Y)$.
Let $P$ be an ideal of this coordinate ring which is prime and different from $\bot$. The assertion
is that there exist $a, b \in K$ such that `W.Equation a b` holds, i.e. $(a,b)$ satisfies the affine
Weierstrass equation of $W$, and such that $P$ is the ideal `XYIdeal W a (C b)`, the ideal generated
by the classes of $X - a$ and of $Y - b$ in the coordinate ring. No smoothness or nonsingularity
assumption on $W$ is imposed, and the prime $P$ is not assumed maximal; maximality is part of what
the proof establishes along the way.

This is Hilbert's Nullstellensatz in the form describing the nonzero points of the spectrum of the
affine coordinate ring of a plane Weierstrass cubic over an algebraically closed field: they are
exactly the vanishing ideals of the affine $K$-points of the curve. It is the basic dictionary
underlying divisors, orders of vanishing and function-field constructions on elliptic curves, and is
invoked in the treatment of isogenies, their dual data and norms, and of period pairs.

